.TH "FORMDATA.KEYS" "3JS" "September 14, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
FormData.keys \- FormData: keys() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]FormData.keys()\f[B]\f[R] method returns an iterator
which iterates through all keys contained in the \f[CR]FormData\f[R].
The keys are strings.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Unlike \f[CR]Map\f[R] keys, \f[CR]FormData\f[R] keys are
not necessarily unique.
A form can contain multiple elements with the same name, so the same key
may appear more than once while iterating.
To retrieve all values associated with a single key, use the
\f[CR]getAll()\f[R] method (or \f[CR]get()\f[R] for only the first
value).
.RE
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
keys()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
An \f[CR]iterator\f[R] of \f[CR]FormData\f[R]\(cqs keys.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] formData = \f[B]new\f[R] FormData();
formData.append(\(dqkey1\(dq, \(dqvalue1\(dq);
formData.append(\(dqkey2\(dq, \(dqvalue2\(dq);

\f[I]// Display the keys\f[R]
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] key \f[B]of\f[R] formData.keys()) {
  console.log(key);
}
.EE
.PP
The result is:
.IP
.EX
key1
key2
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
Using FormData objects
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<Form>\f[R]
